.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt WDPMCD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm wdpmcd
.Nd fan control daemon for the WD My Cloud power management controller
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl f Ar conf
.Op Fl p Ar pidfile
.Op Fl D Ar device
.Nm
.Op Fl f Ar conf
.Op Fl D Ar device
.Fl q | n | s Ar percent
.Nm
.Fl V | h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon keeps a Western Digital My Cloud PR2100 or PR4100 cool.
The fan is driven by the power management controller (PMC), a
microcontroller that is reached over a serial port at 9600 baud.
.Nm
reads the CPU, the disks and the PMC's own temperature, chooses a fan duty
cycle from a configurable curve and writes it back to the PMC.
.Pp
The daemon runs in the foreground and logs to
.Xr syslog 3 .
Use
.Xr daemon 8
or a service manager to run it in the background; the supplied
.Xr rc 8
script and systemd unit do so.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl d
Also log to standard error, including debug messages.
.It Fl D Ar device
Use
.Ar device
as the PMC serial port instead of detecting it.
.It Fl f Ar conf
Read settings from
.Ar conf .
The default is
.Pa /usr/local/etc/wdpmcd.conf
on
.Fx
and
.Pa /etc/wdpmcd.conf
elsewhere.
A missing default file means built-in defaults; a missing file named
with
.Fl f
is an error.
.It Fl h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.It Fl n
Dry run: read all sensors, print them and the duty cycle the curve would
choose, and exit.
Nothing is written to the PMC.
.It Fl p Ar pidfile
Write the process ID to
.Ar pidfile .
.It Fl q
Query: print the fan duty cycle, fan speed and PMC temperature, and exit.
Nothing is written to the PMC.
.It Fl s Ar percent
Set the fan duty cycle once, read it back to confirm it, and exit.
The value must be between 30 and 99.
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Ss Safety
The fan is never commanded below 30 percent.
A request outside 30 to 99 percent is refused before anything is sent.
.Pp
Whenever the daemon cannot trust its picture of the machine it sets the safe
maximum, 99 percent unless
.Cm safe_max
says otherwise, and reads it back.
This happens when
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the PMC is silent, answers with garbage, or answers
.Ql ERR ;
.It
any sensor cannot be read or reports an implausible value;
.It
a reading is older than three intervals;
.It
the fan setting is not confirmed by the read-back; or
.It
the fan turns slower than
.Cm min_rpm .
.El
.Pp
The fan is held at the safe maximum until the cause has gone.
Increases of the duty cycle take effect at once; decreases are limited in
size and rate and need the temperatures to be below the limit by the
hysteresis.
.Pp
On
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGINT
the daemon sets the safe maximum, retries until the PMC confirms it, and
exits.
If it cannot, it exits with a non-zero status.
The
.Xr rc 8
script and systemd unit run
.Nm Fl s Ar 99
once more after the daemon has stopped.
.Sh ARCHITECTURE
.Nm
is a single thread running a single
.Xr poll 2
loop.
It starts no worker threads and never blocks: the serial port is opened
non-blocking, and every external command it depends on
.Pq Xr smartctl 8 , Xr zpool 8 , Xr ifconfig 8 , Xr sysctl 8
runs as a child process whose output is collected from a non-blocking pipe.
Every wait is expressed as a deadline held by the loop.
.Pp
A helper that has not finished by its deadline is killed and reaped with
.Xr waitpid 2
without blocking, and no second command of the same kind is started until it
has been reaped.
Losing a helper costs one reading, and a reading that stays missing long
enough drives the fan up; it can never delay a fan write.
.Pp
The loop re-arms the watchdog on every iteration.
If it stops making progress the kernel ends the process, the supervisor starts
.Nm
again, and start-up sets the safe maximum before anything else.
.Pp
The crate is compiled with
.Ql #![forbid(unsafe_code)]
and contains no
.Ql unsafe
blocks.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width ".Ev WDPMCD_SYSLOG_SOCKET"
.It Ev WDPMCD_SYSLOG_SOCKET
Path of the
.Xr syslogd 8
socket, overriding
.Cm syslog_socket .
The default is
.Pa /var/run/log
on
.Fx
and
.Pa /dev/log
elsewhere.
.El
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv SIGTERM"
.It Dv SIGHUP
Reread the configuration file.
If it is invalid the old settings stay in force.
The device and pidfile are not changed.
.It Dv SIGTERM , SIGINT
Set the safe maximum and exit.
.It Dv SIGALRM
Watchdog.
The loop arms it again on every iteration; the default action, which ends the
process, is left in place.
A loop that wedges therefore dies instead of holding a fan setting it can no
longer revisit.
.It Dv SIGCHLD
Only wakes the loop.
Helper exits are collected when their output pipes reach end-of-file.
.It Dv SIGPIPE
Ignored; a closed syslog socket is reported, not fatal.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /usr/local/etc/wdpmcd.conf" -compact
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/wdpmcd.conf
Configuration on
.Fx .
.It Pa /etc/wdpmcd.conf
Configuration on Linux.
.It Pa /var/run/wdpmcd.pid
Pidfile used by the
.Xr rc 8
script.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Command line errors exit with status 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check that the PMC answers:
.Pp
.Dl # wdpmcd -q
.Pp
Run the fan at 70 percent until the next daemon cycle or restart:
.Pp
.Dl # wdpmcd -s 70
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr wdpmcd.conf 5 ,
.Xr daemon 8 ,
.Xr smartctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in version 0.1.0.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Rei Walker
.Sh CAVEATS
Only the PR2100 has been tested.
The PR4100 and EX4100 use the same PMC protocol and are expected to work.
.Pp
The serial port is locked while the daemon runs, so
.Fl q
and
.Fl s
cannot be used at the same time as the daemon.
